.TH CDN-EGRESS-BILL 1 "2026-11-19" "cdn-egress-bill 1.0.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cdn-egress-bill \- estimate CloudFront egress cost from standard logs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
Schat CloudFront-egressiekosten uit standaardlogbestanden (offline, geen AWS-API).

Usage: cdn-egress-bill [OPTIONS] <LOGS>...

Arguments:
  <LOGS>...  CloudFront-standaardlogbestanden (tekst of gzip)

Options:
      --prices <PRICES>  Prijsbestand. Standaard: de ingebouwde tarieven
      --edges <EDGES>    Edge-kaart. Standaard: de ingebouwde IATA-codes
      --format <FORMAT>  text of json [default: text] [possible values: text, json]
      --month <MONTH>    Alleen deze maand (YYYY-MM)
      --as-of <AS_OF>    Peildatum (YYYY-MM-DD) voor de ouderdom van de prijzen. Standaard: vandaag (UTC)
      --md               Markdown in plaats van tekst of JSON
      --output <FILE>    Schrijf het rapport naar dit bestand in plaats van naar stdout
      --quiet            Alleen het resultaat en fouten; geen waarschuwingen
      --verbose          Voortgang op stderr
      --strict           Exit 3 als er onberekende bytes of verzoeken zijn
  -h, --help             Print help
  -V, --version          Print version
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
cdn-egress-bill reads one or more local CloudFront standard log files and estimates the egress cost per price region and per path.
It reads files only. It makes no network call and uses no AWS SDK.
.PP
A log is gzip when its name ends in
.I .gz
(any case) or its first two bytes are 1f 8b. Otherwise it is UTF-8 text.
A gzip file may hold several members. Each member is read, each member may start with a BOM, and zero bytes after a member are ignored. Other data after a member is an error.
Lines end in LF, CRLF or a lone CR.
The
.B #Fields:
line sets the columns.
.BR date ,
.BR x-edge-location ,
.BR sc-bytes ,
.B cs-uri-stem
and
.B cs-protocol
are required, in any order. A repeated field name is an error.
.PP
The cost comes from
.BR sc-bytes .
Every line counts, also HTTP 404.
The price region comes from the edge location in
.BR x-edge-location :
its first three letters are the IATA code of the edge.
The region is the price region of the edge, not the country of the viewer.
An unknown code is
.BR onbekend
and is not guessed.
China has a separate price list, is not in the map and stays
.BR onbekend .
.PP
One gigabyte is 1073741824 bytes.
The first 1 TB per month is free for all regions together.
The first 10000000 HTTP and HTTPS requests per month are free together.
Each month has its own free allowance.
The built-in rates are in
.I data/prices.txt
and are the only price table.
After the region cost, the amount is shared over the paths in proportion to bytes for data and in proportion to the number of requests for requests.
.PP
The same path given twice as an argument counts twice.
There is no flat-rate, no discount and no Savings Bundle.
The report is an estimate from list prices, not an AWS invoice: free tier, credits and discounts are not included.
Text prints this on one line and labels the cost of each month \fBestimated_usd_month=\fR; JSON starts with \fB"schema_version": 1\fR and has \fB"estimate": true\fR at the top level and \fBestimated_usd_month_micros\fR for each month.
Text shows USD with six decimals. JSON shows micro-USD as an integer (1 USD = 1000000).
Every billable slice is rounded half up to one micro-USD; the sums are not rounded again.
The descriptions in the help text, the report and most messages are in Dutch. The headings and fixed words of the help text (Usage, Options, Print help) and a few messages are in English.
A usage error shows what was typed with control, escape, line-break, bidi and invisible characters written as \eu000a and the like, and an argument that is not UTF-8 as bytes such as \exff.
When the only fault is that an argument is not UTF-8, the message of the argument parser is printed as it is, because it does not repeat the argument.
.PP
A date must be a real day from 1970-01-01 to 2100-12-31, and
.B --month
takes the years 1970 to 2100.
.BR sc-bytes
is a decimal number from 0 to 18446744073709551615.
A log with no data rows is not an error: the report says
.BR "geen verkeer"
and the exit status is 0.
A log is read completely into memory, about 24 times its size for a text log.
A line of #Fields names, a price file and an edge map are checked in time proportional to their size: a #Fields line of 2000000 names was read in under a second, 400000 region lines in a few seconds.
The run time of a price file grows with the number of regions times the number of months in the log: 40000 regions and 1572 months took 13 seconds.
The size limit below counts the file as stored; a gzip file is not limited after unpacking.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --prices \fIFILE\fR
Use this price file instead of the built-in rates.
.TP
.B --edges \fIFILE\fR
Use this edge map instead of the built-in IATA codes.
.TP
.B --format \fItext|json\fR
The output format. The default is
.BR text .
.TP
.B --month \fIYYYY-MM\fR
Keep only this month (UTC).
.TP
.B --as-of \fIYYYY-MM-DD\fR
The date to measure the age of the price file against. The default is today (UTC). When the \fBchecked_on\fR date of the prices (built-in or \fB--prices\fR) is more than 180 days before it, one warning with that date goes to standard error. The exit code stays 0. A value that is not a real date is exit 2.
.TP
.B --md
Print the report as Markdown instead of text or JSON: one table per region list and per path list for each month, with the same amounts as the text report, the estimate line, and \fB|\fR and \fB\e\fR in a cell written as \fB\e|\fR and \fB\e\e\fR. It cannot be combined with \fB--format\fR (exit 2).
.TP
.B --output \fIFILE\fR
Write the report to this file instead of standard output. The report is written to a temporary file in the same directory and renamed over \fIFILE\fR, so the file is the old one or the whole new one. An existing file is replaced; a symbolic link is replaced, not followed; a directory is refused. A missing parent directory, a directory as \fIFILE\fR or a write error is exit 2 with a message. Nothing goes to standard output.
.TP
.B --quiet
Print only the report and errors: the price-age warning is not printed. Exit codes do not change.
.TP
.B --verbose
Also print progress lines on standard error (the price date, each log, the number of records, the number of months). Standard output does not change. \fB--quiet\fR and \fB--verbose\fR together are a usage error (exit 2).
.TP
.B --strict
Print the report, then exit 3 when there are bytes or requests that could not be priced.
.TP
.B -h, --help
Print the usage text.
.TP
.B -V, --version
Print the version.
.PP
A log, a price file or an edge map above 268435456 bytes is refused with exit 2.
A file that is not a regular file is refused with exit 2, except the pipe on standard input, which is counted while it is read and has the same limit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The report was written.
.TP
.B 2
An input or usage error: an invalid month, a missing or unreadable file, a file that is not a regular file, a file over the size limit, an invalid log line, or a standard output that cannot be written.
The message goes to standard error.
.TP
.B 3
.B --strict
was given and some bytes or requests could not be priced. The report is still printed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cdn-egress-bill access.log
cdn-egress-bill --format json --month 2026-10 E1ABC.2026-10-01.gz E1ABC.2026-10-02.gz
cdn-egress-bill --strict --prices my-prices.txt access.log
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
README.md and QUALITY.md in the source tree.
